<p>
    Comparing the types we can compare: a price surprise video earned about twice the like rate of the best-known shock
    video (around 17% against 8%, using the middle of each range) and about four times the like rate of a tiny home that
    viewers thought was overpriced (around 4%). Luxury tours and the one comedy clip land near the price surprise, but
    their counts are older or unconfirmed.
</p>

<h2 id="types">Each type, with TikTok posts to watch</h2>
<p>
    The posts below are public TikToks from the creators and agents behind each type. Counts change daily and creators
    can remove posts, so open them for the current numbers.
</p>

<h3 id="shock">Shock and curiosity homes</h3>
<p>
    A strange, tiny or bizarre home framed as "can you believe this". Comments do the marketing. The best-known example
    is a New York agent's "worst apartment ever", a $1,650 West Village studio, which drew about 20.5 million views
    (<a href="@Sources.FoxNewsWorstApartment.Url" rel="noopener">Fox News</a>). Zillow Gone Wild posts of odd listings
    have reached 24 million (<a href="@Sources.Dwell.Url" rel="noopener">Dwell</a>).
</p>
<ul class="article-examples">
    <li><a href="https://www.tiktok.com/@@newyorkcityrealtor/video/6922923844143500549" rel="noopener">@@newyorkcityrealtor, "worst apartment"</a></li>
    <li><a href="https://www.tiktok.com/@@carolinebassnyc/video/7207526733078170922" rel="noopener">@@carolinebassnyc, a studio with no bathroom inside</a></li>
    <li><a href="https://www.tiktok.com/@@zillowgonewild/video/7337355558858624302" rel="noopener">@@zillowgonewild, a "Blank Check" mansion</a></li>
</ul>
<p>
    Lesson for a listing: the curiosity works, the mockery doesn't. Your seller won't thank you for "worst house ever", but
    "wait until you see the backyard" borrows the same pull.
</p>

<h3 id="creator">Creator with a running bit</h3>
<p>
    One host, one repeatable question. Caleb Simpson stops strangers, asks what they pay in rent, then tours their home;
    his videos average about 10 million views and his celebrity tour reached 41.5 million
    (<a href="@Sources.RollingStone.Url" rel="noopener">Rolling Stone</a>).
</p>
<ul class="article-examples">
    <li><a href="https://www.tiktok.com/@@calebwsimpson/video/7357487981843844395" rel="noopener">@@calebwsimpson, NYC apartment tour</a></li>
    <li><a href="https://www.tiktok.com/@@barbara.corcoran/video/7169237863010405678" rel="noopener">@@barbara.corcoran, apartment tour with Caleb Simpson</a></li>
</ul>
<p>Lesson for a listing: people follow a person. Open every video the same way so viewers recognize you.</p>

<h3 id="speed">Speed and stunt tours</h3>
<p>
    The agent sprints through the house in front of a fixed camera. Trent Miller's speed tours passed 29 million views in a
    month and reached up to 34 million (<a href="@Sources.Nar.Url" rel="noopener">NAR</a>,
    <a href="@Sources.SixAbc.Url" rel="noopener">6abc</a>), and a Kentucky agent's video of dancing through a listing passed
    1.1 million (<a href="@Sources.OwensboroTimes.Url" rel="noopener">Owensboro Times</a>).
</p>
<ul class="article-examples">
    <li><a href="https://www.tiktok.com/@@trent_miller__/video/7376239067333365038" rel="noopener">@@trent_miller__, Maryland speed tour</a></li>
    <li><a href="https://www.tiktok.com/@@trent_miller__/video/7514021825886571819" rel="noopener">@@trent_miller__, $6M San Antonio castle speed tour</a></li>
</ul>

<h3 id="price">Price surprise: "What $X buys in [town]"</h3>
<p>
    A quick walkthrough of a real listing with the price and place as the hook. A UK agency's "What £750,000 buys in
    Leicestershire" drew 1.4 million views and 173,300 likes (<a href="@Sources.Negotiator.Url" rel="noopener">The Negotiator</a>),
    and a London agent's townhouse drew 1.4 million views and about 267,000 likes
    (<a href="@Sources.PropertyIndustryEye.Url" rel="noopener">Property Industry Eye</a>). In Texas, a $366,500 new build
    reached about 7 million views in three days, mostly from Canadians amazed at the price
    (<a href="@Sources.NarcityAlamo.Url" rel="noopener">Narcity</a>).
</p>
<ul class="article-examples">
    <li><a href="https://www.tiktok.com/@@hortons.co/video/7280574057404239136" rel="noopener">@@hortons.co, "What £635,000 buys in south Leicestershire"</a></li>
    <li><a href="https://www.tiktok.com/@@hortons.co/video/7277886788247571744" rel="noopener">@@hortons.co, "£550,000 buys this house"</a></li>
    <li><a href="https://www.tiktok.com/@@tanyabaker.co.uk/video/7329651000204135713" rel="noopener">@@tanyabaker.co.uk, "Would you live here?" Brockley, £1,750,000</a></li>
    <li><a href="https://www.tiktok.com/@@hortons.co/video/7310259634487446817" rel="noopener">@@hortons.co, "Can you guess how much this property is listed for"</a></li>
</ul>
<p>
    Lesson for a listing: the price has to surprise the people watching, not your local buyers. The Texas tours went viral
    with viewers from another country. Frame the price for outsiders, and try holding it back for a "guess the price"
    ending to draw comments.
</p>

<h3 id="luxury">Luxury and mansion tours</h3>
<p>
    Big homes, big prices, a smooth walkthrough set to music. One Los Angeles agent's account passed 110 million views in
    2020, with single tours at 5.5 million and 48.5 million
    (<a href="@Sources.Inman2020.Url" rel="noopener">Inman</a>).
</p>
<ul class="article-examples">
    <li><a href="https://www.tiktok.com/@@aarongrushowhomes/video/7260220075834101035" rel="noopener">@@aarongrushowhomes, $47.8M Malibu estate</a></li>
    <li><a href="https://www.tiktok.com/@@aarongrushowhomes/video/6792367383618112774" rel="noopener">@@aarongrushowhomes, "The One"</a></li>
</ul>

<h3 id="comedy">Comedy skit tours</h3>
<p>
    The agent plays a character or parodies a show while touring. One Atlanta agent's clip reached 7 million views
    (<a href="@Sources.InmanGlennda.Url" rel="noopener">Inman</a>). The Close found no strong evidence yet that skits sell
    homes faster than a normal tour (<a href="@Sources.TheClose.Url" rel="noopener">The Close, 2026</a>).
</p>
<ul class="article-examples">
    <li><a href="https://www.tiktok.com/@@tampa_bre/video/7493893020782284063" rel="noopener">@@tampa_bre, "you pulled up in an F150"</a></li>
    <li><a href="https://www.tiktok.com/@@chandler.realtor/video/7417134923959749934" rel="noopener">@@chandler.realtor, "Not so typical home tour"</a></li>
</ul>

<h3 id="price-shock">Price shock, the wrong way</h3>
<p>
    A tiny or overpriced home that viewers argue about. A San Antonio agent's tour of a tiny home in a new community drew
    about 500,000 views and nearly 20,000 likes early on (<a href="@Sources.SaCurrent.Url" rel="noopener">San Antonio Current</a>),
    with many comments saying their car had more space. It gets attention, but most of it is negative.
</p>
<ul class="article-examples">
    <li><a href="https://www.tiktok.com/@@trent_miller__/video/7488360063413865774" rel="noopener">@@trent_miller__, "Would you buy a tiny home?"</a></li>
    <li><a href="https://www.tiktok.com/@@contact_corona/video/7259368035444690219" rel="noopener">@@contact_corona, "Overpriced or not?"</a></li>
</ul>

<h3 id="neighborhood">Neighborhood and "living in" tours</h3>
<p>
    The home framed by the neighborhood around it. We found no single video with published counts, so this type has the
    weakest evidence here, but one Washington, D.C. team built an account with 61 million views on home and neighborhood
    tours (<a href="@Sources.Spokesman.Url" rel="noopener">Spokesman-Review</a>).
</p>
<ul class="article-examples">
    <li><a href="https://www.tiktok.com/@@heider_realestate/video/7633854193727474975" rel="noopener">@@heider_realestate, "Would you move to Georgetown to live here?"</a></li>
    <li><a href="https://www.tiktok.com/@@heider_realestate/video/7482549908290014494" rel="noopener">@@heider_realestate, "POV: you're a guest at our invite-only listing preview"</a></li>
</ul>

<h2 id="takeaways">What to take from this for your next listing</h2>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Lead with the price and the place</strong> when you want likes and serious viewers. It is the type with the strongest like rates we could verify.</li>
    <li><strong>Open on the standout room</strong> when you want reach. Curiosity drives views; save the mockery for someone else's listing.</li>
    <li><strong>Be the same person every time</strong> if you want followers. The biggest numbers belong to creators with a repeatable bit.</li>
    <li><strong>Add your announcement on top.</strong> Just listed, Open house and Price drop work as a banner on one of the types above, not as the whole video.</li>
    <li><strong>Measure your own posts.</strong> Write down views, likes, shares and leads after two and seven days, by type. That will tell you more than any viral example.</li>
</ul>
<p>
    Creative Listing Studio's vertical videos already cover several of these: a price and place hook, an opening on your
    standout room, an AI presenter for a hosted feel, and a cinematic tour with a drone fly-in for luxury homes.
</p>

